## Milestone

M4 (author, 2026-09-29: "Zotero storage smells like M4 work"). Do not start
before the milestone opens.

## Context

Tracker. Author decision (2026-09-29, Imagine conversation): the JETP
observatory should not maintain its own document management. Zotero offers a
stable data model and a plugin ecosystem, and the author cites these documents
from Zotero anyway (EDM rule), so any other store would make two catalogues of
the same items. A due-diligence check of Python alternatives (papis, DataLad /
git-annex, Paperless-ngx, Mayan EDMS, Calibre) found none better for this role.

Today step D1 is a home-made document manager: `data/jetp/documents.csv`
(392 rows), `snapshots.csv`, and the content-addressed object store
`data/jetp/documents/objects/<sha256>` under DVC (padme remote, about 282 MB).

## Division of labour

- Zotero (a dedicated group library, separate from the author's personal
  library, cloud-synced for distribution) holds document identity (title, publisher, date,
  language, type, URL), files (one attachment per byte version), edition and
  translation links, reading, annotation and citation.
- The repo keeps what is research data, not document management:
  `retrievals.csv`, `publisher-link-checks.csv`, `web-archive-captures.csv`,
  `decisions.md`, and steps D2-D4.

## Design constraints

1. The existing `document_id` goes into each Zotero item's Extra field, so the
   D2-D4 keys stay unchanged.
2. The pipeline never reads live Zotero. It reads a committed export of the
   group library (Web API, or a Better BibTeX auto-export), pinned like any
   other input.
3. Every locator records the hash of the file it read. A check flags an
   attachment whose bytes changed. A new publisher version becomes a new
   attachment on the same item.
4. DVC shrinks to a hash-keyed cache filled from Zotero, plus the frozen
   evidence for each release. It stops being a second system of record.
5. The group is PublicClosed: an open public group allows no file sharing,
   while a closed-membership public group shares metadata with everyone and
   attachments with members only (author, 2026-09-29; details in 1511), so
   the files stay
   non-redistributed, as in `docs/jetp-storage.md` ("not served wholesale").
   Public access to the documents themselves already goes through the Web
   Archive snapshots (`web-archive-captures.csv`).

## Design decisions (author, 2026-09-29)

To settle before the pilot, so that 1511 does not settle them by accident.

1. **Scope: documents, not data.** Zotero holds what a person reads or cites
   (plans, reports, agreements, project pages). Machine data fetched from
   APIs stays in DVC: `crs-bulk.dvc` (81 files, 41.8 MB), `recovery.dvc`
   (one file, 210 MB), and the IATI JSON, XML and CSV payloads.
2. **Versions.** A new edition (`edition_of`) or a translation
   (`translation_of`) is its own Zotero item, linked by "Related". A byte
   change with no editorial change (republished file, new metadata) is one
   more attachment on the same item, its title carrying the retrieval date.
3. **Review.** The harvester writes new items into an inbox collection; the
   author reviews in Zotero and tags reviewed items `reviewed`. The export
   keeps only reviewed items, which replaces the "reviewed identities" role of
   `documents.csv`.
4. **Publishers.** Party links (`document-publishers.csv` to `parties.csv`,
   with role) stay in the repo, keyed by `document_id`. Zotero gets a display
   name only.
5. **Observatory links.** The group's public item page becomes the document
   link on the observatory. It replaces, not supplements, the current
   "Publisher's page" and "Web Archive copy" links: the Zotero record carries
   the publisher URL (URL field) and the Web Archive capture (Archive fields
   or a link attachment). Page locators (`#page=N`) need a direct file URL,
   which a Zotero item page cannot give: document-level links go to the
   Zotero record, page-level links go to the raw Web Archive copy (the `id_`
   form of the capture URL) plus `#page=N`, never to the publisher's live
   URL. The pilot tests that form on one of our captures.
6. **Where the files are.** Master copy in Zotero's cloud storage, in the
   group library, counted against the author's unlimited quota. Reading
   copies in each desktop client's data directory, synced by Zotero. The
   pipeline's copy on padme is a cache keyed by hash, filled through the Web
   API into the same path as today (`data/jetp/documents/objects/<sha256>`),
   so the parsers do not change. Releases freeze the files they cite in DVC on
   padme, as evidence independent of Zotero.
7. **Sync direction.** Two writers into Zotero (the harvester creates items
   and attachments; the author reviews, edits and annotates), one reader (the
   pipeline). The repo never pushes metadata back to Zotero. Edits are watched
   by polling, not live: a scheduled pass on the runner of [[1184]] asks the
   Web API for what changed since the last library version
   (`?since=<version>`, plus deletions), regenerates the export, and opens a
   PR when it differs, so every Zotero edit reaches the pipeline as a
   reviewable diff. An attachment of a cited file whose checksum changed
   raises an alarm instead of a silent update.
8. **Hashes.** SHA-256 stays the key of locators and of the cache; it is
   written into each attachment (title or child note), and the cache checks
   it after every download. Zotero's own checksum (MD5, to confirm in the
   pilot) only detects changes on the Zotero side.
9. **Manual collection through the Zotero Connector.** Documents behind a
   login or a bot wall, today collected by `jetp-harvest-blocked` (author's
   Firefox session) or `jetp-collect-downloads`, are saved with the browser
   Connector straight into the inbox, with URL and access date. The
   `browser-manual` collection method (see [[1180]]) becomes "saved with the
   Connector"; the harvester then fills in the SHA-256 and the retrieval row.
10. **Link durability.** Observatory links are generated from the export, so
    one build re-points them if zotero.org URLs change. The group gets a
    second admin, so the catalogue does not hang on one account. The public
    release ships the export (CSL-JSON or RDF), so the catalogue survives
    without Zotero.
11. **Multilingual titles.** Zotero has no parallel-title fields: the
    original-language title goes in Title, the English translation in Extra,
    with Language set. Settled before the pilot, which is on Viet Nam.
12. **Annotations (later, beyond M4).** Annotations stay in Zotero and are
    not exported. A highlighted figure could later become an observation (D3)
    with its page and hash (AEDIST territory); nothing in this design should
    close that door.

## Children

- 1511: pilot on Viet Nam (58 documents, the smallest country).
- Later children are filed after the pilot's findings: the remaining
  countries, switching the harvester (`corpus_harvest_ledger.py`) to write
  through the Web API (reuse the `zotero-import` / `index-source` machinery),
  and retiring `documents.csv` as a hand-edited table.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every JETP document lives in the group library, with its `document_id`
      in Extra.
- [ ] The pipeline builds D1-D4 from the committed export, with no read of
      live Zotero.
- [ ] The D2-D4 outputs are byte-identical before and after the migration;
      the observatory differs only in its document links (decision 5).
- [ ] Integration review done (`/roar` step 8).
